ialized centers)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes explicit escalation routes to specialized clinicians for hematologic malignancies: the patient is encouraged to consider consulting both an AML SPECIALIST and a CML SPECIALIST for expert opinion and guidance, reflecting a model where complex cases may warrant subspecialty input beyond routine care. [Data: Entities (1731, 931); Relationships (2879, 1408)]\\n\\nThere is also a location-based specialization prompt: the patient is encouraged to ask about a nearby cancer center or hospital specializing in anal cancer. This indicates that facility specialization is treated as a meaningful factor in care quality and decision-making, complementing clinician specialization. [Data: Entities (6338); Relationships (14499)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical trial participation is a formal decision point supported by informed discussion and social support\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The patient explicitly asks about JOINING A CLINICAL TRIAL, including what is available and whether they are eligible, establishing clinical research participation as a recognized pathway within care planning. This is positioned as a question-driven choice rather than an automatic recommendation, consistent with shared decision-making themes. [Data: Entities (6383); Relationships (14579)]\\n\\nThe decision is also socially scaffolded: the patient may discuss trial participation and consent information with trusted family and friends, and the community includes the notion of an ADVOCATE (either the patient or someone close) to help speak up and ensure needs are addressed. [Data: Entities (4252, 4353); Relationships (8886, 9093)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Care delivery imposes logistical burdens that are explicitly anticipated (sessions, preparation, work/school, return to activities)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Several entities represent the operational reality of treatment: the patient asks how many TREAT